In <1knss1$r2n@pith.uoregon.edu> chrisd@darkwing.uoregon.edu (Christian Delay) writes:

>Does anyone have any tips on compiling nh3.1 on a macintosh using Think C 5.0?
>I don't have access to MPW...

1) Turn on far data and far code
2) Turn on 4-byte ints
3) Look in the MPW make file for the right files to use
4) Make the MakeDefs application, and run it with all the options
   You will probably need to use cconsole for this
   You can do this by bundling all source files needed into one
   segment and go.
5) Make the lev_comp application, in the same way as MakeDefs.
   Run for all lever files. (tedious!)
6) Make the dgn_comp application, and run in the same way.
7) Make the "real" NetHack by bundling all needed files (all of
   src, sys/share/random.c and all of sys/mac if memory serves
   me right) into one segment and compile.
8) Make NetHack.rsrc by decoding the .hqx files, run SARez on
   the Rez file, and add NetHack Sounds.rsrc to it.

9) Congrats! Set the memory to > 1.5 MB for this version, though,
   since all code will be in one segment.

I have not tried this, however, it should work. Try defining
macintosh and FAR_CODE to 1 in your prefix for automagic configuration.
Steps 4 and 5 are the ones that deter us from doing a Think C port
ourselves (the MPW make script makes compilation much faster, since
we don't have to switch between 4 different applications)

